  <div class="callout callout--note">
    <span class="callout__heading">⚠ Important — please read first</span>
    <p>
      UAE immigration rules change regularly and vary based on your nationality, employer
      and circumstances. This is a general overview only — <strong>not legal advice</strong>.
      Always verify current requirements with the
      <a href="https://gdrfad.gov.ae" target="_blank" rel="noopener">General Directorate of Residency and Foreigners Affairs (GDRFA)</a>
      or an MOHRE-registered immigration consultant before making decisions.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p>
    The UAE visa system is very different from the UK or Europe. Most residence visas in Dubai
    are sponsored — meaning someone else (your employer, a company you own, or the government
    scheme you qualify under) applies on your behalf. You don't typically walk in and apply for
    residency off your own back. Understanding which route applies to you is the essential
    first step.
  </p>

  <h2>Routes at a glance</h2>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Employer-sponsored work permit</strong> — you have a job offer from a UAE company</li>
    <li><strong>Golden Visa</strong> — 10-year residency for investors, entrepreneurs and exceptional talent</li>
    <li><strong>Green Visa</strong> — 5-year self-sponsored residency for skilled employees and freelancers</li>
    <li><strong>Remote Work Visa</strong> — 1-year visa to work remotely for a non-UAE employer</li>
    <li><strong>Investor/Business Owner Visa</strong> — if you own a company registered in the UAE</li>
    <li><strong>Retirement Visa</strong> — 5-year residency for qualifying retirees</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Employer-sponsored work permit</h2>

  <p>
    The most common route into Dubai. If you have a job offer from a UAE-registered company,
    your employer applies for a work permit and residence visa on your behalf. You don't
    initiate this process — your company's PRO (Public Relations Officer) or HR team handles it.
  </p>

  <p>The typical process:</p>
  <ol>
    <li>You accept a job offer and sign your contract</li>
    <li>Your employer obtains an entry permit allowing you to enter the UAE</li>
    <li>You enter Dubai on this entry permit</li>
    <li>You complete a <strong>medical fitness test</strong> (blood test and chest X-ray, done at approved centres)</li>
    <li>You register your <strong>Emirates ID</strong> biometrics</li>
    <li>Your residence visa is stamped into your passport</li>
    <li>Your Emirates ID card arrives by post — this is your main ID document in the UAE</li>
  </ol>

  <div class="callout callout--tip">
    <span class="callout__heading">✦ Emirates ID</span>
    <p>
      Your Emirates ID is essential for almost everything in Dubai — opening a bank account,
      signing a tenancy contract, accessing government services and healthcare. Collect it as
      soon as it's ready after your visa is issued. Keep it with you at all times.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Golden Visa</h2>

  <p>
    The UAE Golden Visa offers <strong>10-year renewable residency</strong> and is one of the
    most generous long-term visa schemes in the world. It's designed to attract investors,
    entrepreneurs, outstanding students and exceptional talent across specific fields.
  </p>

  <p>Who can apply:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Investors</strong> — real estate investors (minimum AED 2 million property value), or investors in UAE businesses meeting certain thresholds</li>
    <li><strong>Entrepreneurs</strong> — founders of an established startup or with a project approved by an accredited business incubator</li>
    <li><strong>Exceptional talent</strong> — recognised specialists in science, medicine, arts, culture, sports and specific technical fields</li>
    <li><strong>Outstanding students</strong> — high-achieving students from UAE and international universities</li>
    <li><strong>Highly skilled professionals</strong> — certain specialisations with a minimum salary threshold (check current requirements on official UAE government portals)</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Golden Visa holders can sponsor family members and don't need an employer sponsor.
    It's a genuine long-term residency option for those who qualify.
  </p>

