  <h2>Green Visa</h2>

  <p>
    Introduced in 2022, the <strong>Green Visa offers 5-year self-sponsored residency</strong>
    — meaning you don't need an employer to sponsor you. It's designed for:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Skilled employees</strong> — working for a UAE company with a minimum monthly salary (check current threshold on the official portal) and holding a relevant professional qualification</li>
    <li><strong>Freelancers and self-employed</strong> — with a UAE freelance permit and minimum annual income requirement</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    The Green Visa is particularly useful for people who want more independence from a single
    employer — if you lose your job, you have a grace period to find new employment rather
    than having to leave immediately as under a standard work permit.
  </p>

  <h2>Remote Work Visa</h2>

  <p>
    Dubai's Remote Work Visa (sometimes called the Virtual Working Programme) allows you to
    live in Dubai for <strong>one year</strong> while working remotely for a company or as
    a freelancer based outside the UAE. You pay no UAE income tax on your foreign-earned income.
  </p>

  <p>Requirements typically include:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Proof of remote employment or self-employment with an income above a minimum threshold</li>
    <li>Valid health insurance</li>
    <li>A clean criminal record</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    The visa is not currently renewable in the same category — after one year, you'd need to
    switch to another visa type to stay long-term. Check current availability and requirements
    as this scheme has evolved since its launch.
  </p>

  <h2>Key things everyone should know</h2>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>No income tax</strong> — personal income tax is 0% in the UAE. VAT is 5% on most goods and services.</li>
    <li><strong>Medical test is mandatory</strong> for most residence visas — it screens for specific communicable diseases. A positive result can result in deportation in some circumstances.</li>
    <li><strong>Visa cancellation when leaving employment</strong> — under a standard work permit, if you leave your job, your employer cancels your visa and you typically have a grace period (check current rules) to find new employment or leave.</li>
    <li><strong>Alcohol</strong> — legal for non-Muslim residents (and tourists) in licensed venues. You need a liquor licence to purchase from shops.</li>
    <li><strong>Pork products</strong> — available in specific sections of supermarkets, clearly marked.</li>
  </ul>

